\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Weaker Late; Seen Stable Despite Baht Move \b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1997\par 11:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is slightly weaker but mostly stable against the U.S. dollar in late Asian dealings Wednesday despite the earlier volatile movements in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a sell-off in the Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  The sharp fall in the baht early Wednesday triggered a flight of funds towards safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar. This drove the U.S. dollar to an intraday low of S$1.4320 at 0200 GMT (10:00 p.m. EDT). \par \par \uc2  Amid some confusion and unconfirmed rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore - the country's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - was seen defending the U.S. dollar against the Singapore dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced back up in very thin trading. The U.S. dollar has been holding steady against the Singapore dollar since, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The baht was the center of attention in Southeast Asia Wednesday as speculators drove the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower against the U.S. dollar. The move prompted the central banks of Thailand and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support the Thai baht, said a joint statement from both agencies. \par \par \uc2  'The market doesn't seem interested in the Sing dollar now,' said a senior trader at a major Singapore bank. 'People might be afraid to sell the (U.S.) dollar for fear of some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' at lower levels, the trader said. \par \par \uc2  'We think the dollar will be stuck between S$1.4340 and S$1.4380 for the time being,' said another trader at a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  Around 0920 GMT (5:20 a.m. EDT), the dollar is trading around S$1.4363, up from S$1.4350 around 0255 GMT (10:55 p.m. EDT), and up from S$1.4348 from 0000 GMT (8:00 p.m. EDT) early in Asian trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at S$1.4349 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0947GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5e03fmm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
