\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand/Singapore/Central Banks-2: Other Measures Cited \b0\par\par\uc2 151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1997\par 10:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--The central banks of Thailand and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in tandem Wednesday to defend the Thai baht against a big speculative attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Thailand said. \par \par \uc2  'This concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at stemming excessive speculative activities which may lead to disorderly conditions in the Thai and regional financial markets,' the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement Wednesday. 'Other appropriate measures will be taken as necessary.' \par \par \uc2  The attack on the baht, in which foreign institutional investors dumped baht in favor of dollars, was the worst in three months. It pushed the U.S. dollar up to a high of 26.26 baht in early trading Wednesday, up from 25.915 baht at the close of trading Tuesday. Around 0757 GMT, the dollar was at 26.09 baht. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0804GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5e03eu1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
