\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Thai baht leads regionals lower. \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 1997\par 07:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 14 (Reuter) - Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  came under assault from foreign fund sales on Wednesday, led early by dumping of the Thai baht, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Asian morning session was highlighted by a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading to flight-to-quality buying, they said \par \par \uc2  Rising concerns about the slowing Thai economy and rumours of political turmoil within the cabinet, seen hampering effective monetary policy, triggered baht sales for dollar. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 26.000/26.050 to the dollar compared with 25.900/25.910 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Bank of Thailand fixing the mid-rate at 25.86 did not prevent the rate slipping to 26.03 \par \par \uc2  "There was a lot of stop-loss and panic-selling which caused the market to trade 20 satangs above the fixing," a dealer with a U.S. bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit continued to weaken pressured by ringgit/Sing sales and despite alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Foreign funds have been aggressive in ringgit/Sing as well, which brought Bank Negara in around the 20 level," said another dealer based in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara was said to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2.5220 using large local and foreign banks to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  At 0430 GMT, the ringgit held at 2.5230/40 against an early 2.5208 level. \par \par \uc2  It fell to as low as 2.5237, its lowest level since January 6 when it traded at 2.5235. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started its meltdown on Tuesday largely due to commercial dollar buying thought to be related to a major telecommunications deal in which a U.S.-Malaysia consortium had to pay US$1 billion to South Africa's telecommunications authority, Telkom. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also seen stepping in to prevent the rise of the Singapore dollar amid dollar demand and ringgit sales, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rise of the Sing dollar initially was also attributed to flight from other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar was at 1.4355/65 against the U.S. dollar compared with 1.4330 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said generally weak ringgit/Sing cross, hovering around 56.86/90, was also lending support. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the baht also spilled over to Indonesia's rupiah, hurt by ample supply and jitters over an election campaign which held some potential for political disturbance. \par \par \uc2  Support, however, was seen coming from tighter liquidity later this week due to demand for weekly tax payments on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,433.50/4.50 at 0430 GMT against an opening of 2,430. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221-384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662-652-0642) and Jacqueline Wong in Singapore \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5e05km8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
