\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Ringgit hit by dollar buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1997\par 06:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 13 (Reuter) - The Malaysian ringgit opened weak and lost more ground in Tuesday morning trade, largely due to commercial dollar buying said to be related to a major telecommunications deal. \par \par \uc2  "There was a lot of commercial dollar buying out of KL. We'll be trying to break the 2.5150 level," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the ringgit slid overnight and remained under pressure on Tuesday on reports that a U.S.-Malaysia consortium had to pay over US$1 billion to South Africa's state telecommunications authority, Telkom, by next week. \par \par \uc2  Telekom Malaysia Bhd and U.S.-based SBC Communications Inc are buying a 30 percent stake in Telkom. \par \par \uc2  The deal is due to be signed on Wednesday in Cape Town, after which there will be an exchange of cash for shares and a new board will take over management of the company. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 2.5130/40 to the dollar at 0405 GMT against 2.5085/95 at the close of Kuala Lumpur trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer said the dollar could target the 2.5180 level but players were likely to be cautious around 2.5150/60 as this was the level at which Bank Negara Malaysia last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was near the lower end of its new 1.4330-1.4380 range against the U.S. dollar, but dealers said it would be underpinned by a drop in the ringgit/Sing cross. \par \par \uc2  "Ringgit/Sing has broken 0.5730. It could go down to 0.5680 now," the dealer said. The cross was at 0.5713/19 at 0405 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht weakened on heavy short-covering of the dollar as the U.S. unit showed a firmer tone against the majors. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 25.925/25.930 to the dollar at 0405 GMT against 25.910/25.920 late on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the mid-rate at 25.88 per dollar versus 25.85 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "There was heavy buying of the dollar this morning. Most players were severely short of the dollar following its rebound yesterday," a dealer at a European bank in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  "Singaporean players also tried to push the dollar/baht higher as they think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the mid-rate relatively low," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said heavy buying of the dollar squeezed liquidity in the banking system as commercial banks were retaining funds to speculate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah was largely steady as the market focused on liquidity, expected to tighten this week, with weekly tax payments due Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was at 2,427.70/28.20 at 0405 GMT against an opening of 2,427.0/28.0. \par \par \uc2  A Singapore dealer said the market was short in the forward market and long on dollars on the spot side, reflected by continuously high tom/next swap rates recently. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said going long on dollars appeared to be justified due to uncertainty during the campaign for the May 29 general elections. But others said it was not profitable because of expensive daily roll-over costs. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221-384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662-652-0642) and Sonali Desai in Singapore \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5d05r51\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
