\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore dollar seen heading for two-year low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par 10:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 20 (Reuter) - The normally unflappable Singapore dollar is set to plumb lows not seen in over two years against the U.S. dollar as the city-state struggles in an export slump and funds flood into the Malaysian ringgit. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the Singapore dollar fell to a low of 1.4443 from just below 1.4400 to the U.S. dollar on Thursday after the government announced an unexpected drop in non-oil domestic exports for February. \par \par \uc2  They said the move put the Singapore dollar, which has weakened steadily since starting the year just shy of 1.4000, firmly on track to 1.4500, a level not seen since February 1995. "The market has been building up for dollar/Sing to test 1.4500 and ringgit/Sing to test 0.5850 for a few months, so we're almost there," said a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted at 1.4438/48 to the U.S. dollar in late Asian trade on Thursday. The ringgit was at a 30-month high of 58.30/36 Singapore cents against 55.37 at the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  The Trade Development Board said Singapore's non-oil domestic exports fell 7.9 percent year-on-year in February. A Reuters survey of 16 economists earlier in the week produced a average forecast of a 1.5 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said massive fund flows into the higher yielding Malaysian ringgit, backed by the country's positive economic outlook, were the driving force behind the Singapore dollar's weakness against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Daniel Lian, chief strategist, Asian bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at NatWest Markets, said the market had pushed the ringgit to a level that was technically bullish, helping perpetuate long positions. \par \par \uc2  "In the past few weeks, we have seen another few yards (billions) of long ringgit positions being added to the market both on the ringgit/sing crosses and versus the U.S. dollar," Lian told Reuters Financial Television. \par \par \uc2  He said the exchange rate could consolidate around the 58.30/40 region for a while but an eventual push to 60 Singapore cents was likely this year. \par \par \uc2  Expressions of unconcern by Singapore authorities about their dollar's fall against the U.S. dollar have also contributed to its descent, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when we were at 1.4300 and now we have decisively broken into territory that nobody expected dollar/sing to be traded to," Lian said. \par \par \uc2  P.K. Basu, director of regional macroeconomics at Union Bank of Switzerland, said sluggish export growth, coupled with subdued inflation, had led to a marginal shift in Singapore's unstated policy of maintaining a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said domestic factors as well as the U.S. dollar's strength against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had led to a "policy response from Singapore authorities aimed at weakening the Sing dollar against the strong U.S. dollar". \par \par \uc2  But Jacqueline Ong, regional economist at research firm I.D.E.A., said Singapore was unlikely to condone any sustained weakness in its dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think 1.4500 is quite overdone because the government still basically favours a long-term uptrend on the Sing dollar to curb imported inflation," Ong said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt3k08fum\par