\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up But Off Early Highs After Fin Min Comments \b0\par\par\uc2 169 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1997\par 03:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (AP-Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is up in early trading Wednesday against the U.S. dollar, but below an early high to which it climbed after Finance Minister Richard Hu reiterated, in a newspaper report, that the government favors a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb imported inflation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Hu's comments caught the market long of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which fell sharply to S$1.4060 in around 0037 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is at S$1.4082 around 0215 GMT, down from S$1.4097 in early Asian dealings. \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that the U.S. dollar's mild recovery might have been helped by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose stated policy is to curb any unwanted appreciation that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows. The MAS doesn't comment on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 29-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0238GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt1t00hf2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
