\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Regionals sag as dollar roars ahead. \b0\par\par\uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 29 (Reuter) - The U.S. dollar's relentless climb to a 47-month high of 122 yen in Asian trade dominated activity in most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 121.83/88 yen in Tokyo at 0445 GMT after rising above 122 yen earlier. \par \par \uc2  Only the typically stoic Singapore dollar stood its ground against the U.S. unit, boosted by a newspaper report quoting the city-state's finance minister saying he expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate this year. \par \par \uc2  In an interview published by the Financial Times in its Wednesday edition, Richard Hu was quoted as saying some strengthening of the Sing dollar would be desirable to keep inflation down. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market participants reacted to the report by unwinding long Malaysian ringgit/Sing dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  But they said active buying of the U.S. dollar around the S$1.4060 level by local banks sparked speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing players to back off. \par \par \uc2  The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets through local banks. \par \par \uc2  "Some people suspect that our authorities might be down there picking up (the U.S. dollar) because they can't have the Sing that strong either," said a senior dealer at an Asian bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar was likely to remain above S$1.4060 for the day but would have trouble rising above a large number of sell orders around S$1.4100. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the article will have an impact over the medium term...the trend of the ringgit/Sing is still higher," said a Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said the cross was likely to stay supported around 0.5620 and face resistance at 0.5660 for the day. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah was also relatively firm as players remained concerned about a possible liquidity squeeze. \par \par \uc2  Some Jakarta dealers said the early liquidity concern was exaggerated. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think liquidity is tight. It's merely that people are rushing to square overnight swap positions which triggered overnight money to increase," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A local bank dealer said offshore operators were seen entering the local market late on Tuesday, buying up tom/next swaps to a high 0.65/0.70 points. \par \par \uc2  "But local operators could not square the entire exposure, while some were speculating for a lower rate on Wednesday as overnight money fell to 10.0 percent late on Tuesday," he said. \par \par \uc2  "As everybody moves to cover his short position early this morning, rates are being driven up. I think short-term rates will decline later because there is sufficient supply at present," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said overall sentiment on the rupiah remained firm as operators focused on a possible tightening in the money market on month-end factors, the lunar new year and the Eid al-Fitr celebrations in early February. \par \par \uc2  The local bank dealer said a temporary slowdown in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rupiah depreciation policy and weekly swap costs of some 3.5 points caused operators to go short in dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht slid against the dollar as the U.S. unit's surge forced the Bank of Thailand to fix the baht/dollar mid-rate at a 6-1/2-year low of 25.86. It was at 25.81 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 25.890-25.895 to the dollar at 0445 GMT against 25.845-25.865 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar/baht forward positions were being bought up, partly on concern about some Thai economic data due to be released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "With all the buying of dollars, the market needs to unwind its outright positions now. People took these positions early and failed to hedge them. They're now feeling the pinch because what they gain on interest may not cover the loss in terms of the exchange rate," said the Asian bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit came under pressure from the rising dollar/majors and from players unwinding their ringgit/Sing positions. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said it was unlikely to weaken beyond 2.50 to the U.S. dollar for the day as market sentiment still favoured the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I don't see dollar/ringgit going much higher even with the strong dollar/majors...Of all the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Malaysia has got the best fundamentals at the moment," said the Singapore-based dealer. \par \par \uc2  -- Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (62-21-384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662) 652-0642 and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703090) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dt1t02i22\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
