\par\par \b \uc2  Japan signs repo pacts with seven Asian nations. \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 1996\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 25 (Reuter) - Japan banded together with seven Asian partners on Thursday in a drive to provide liquidity and help stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the region. \par \par \uc2  Japan's monetary authorities said they had signed a set of bilateral accords called repurchase agreements, or "repo pacts", with Australia, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand, that went into effect on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The pacts give participants access to immediate cash -- for use to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against excessive volatility -- by selling their holdings of U.S. government securities to a pact partner on a temporary basis with a promise to buy them back later. \par \par \uc2  Asian countries began mulling such arrangements after the 1994 Mexican peso crisis raised the spectre of similar speculative attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fast-growing Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped after the announcement, trading at just above 107 yen in Tokyo mid-afternoon trade after closing at around 106.56 yen in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said that while the pacts would certainly provide some support for the dollar in the short term, they were unlikely to be a long-term factor. \par \par \uc2  "For now, its greatest value is to emphasise the cooperative relationship between Asian monetary authorities," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  A Finance Ministry official told reporters the arrangements were meant to increase cooperation and were not specifically aimed at boosting the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In November, Australia, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand -- mindful of the potential economic damage that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility can inflict -- signed similar agreements. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then said it would do so with Indonesia and Singapore, adding to the bilateral accords it forged last year with Hong Kong, Malaysia and Thailand. \par \par \uc2  In February, the Finance Ministry unveiled a pact allowing Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask its counterparts in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hong Kong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf in dollar/yen trade in their markets, a move that some in the ministry were keen to portray as a step toward closer Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry said in its statement announcing the pacts on Thursday that Japan's monetary authorities believed the latest moves would "further enhance mutual cooperation amongst Asian monetary authorities". \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have said regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms like the repurchase pacts would be useful supplements to the leading role played by Washington and Tokyo in stabilising the key dollar/yen exchange rate and to that played by the Group of Seven leading industrialised nations. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4p08xhe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
