\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Ringgit brushes aside deficit woes. \b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 9 (Reuter) - The Malaysian ringgit was untouched by a sharp rise in the January trade deficit and remained rangebound on Tuesday amid growing market hopes of an interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the announcement by Malaysia late on Monday of a 1.1 billion ringgit trade deficit in January had little impact as the market was already short ringgit. \par \par \uc2  "The figure was largely discounted by the market. The interest rate hikes are seen as positive for the Malaysian economy," said a dealer with a Singapore bank. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia is widely rumoured to be planning interest rate hikes through a combination of a hike in banks' statutory reserve requirements and open market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Negara. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit was at 2.5320/30 to the dollar at midday against 2.5319/25 at the close of local markets on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht eased to 25.273-25.278 to the dollar against 25.247-25.250 late on Friday on a firmer dollar abroad and offshore demand for the American unit, dealers in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  Thai money and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were closed for a public holiday on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is about to test 108 yen again," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. "Singaporean banks are actively buying dollars (against baht) this morning to cover their dollar short positions." \par \par \uc2  A daily market commentary by consulting firm I.D.E.A. in Singapore said the baht could suffer following reports that the Bank of Thailand was considering changes to its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  management system in the face of continuing inflows of short-term capital. \par \par \uc2  The Thai overnight interbank rate rose to 6.25-6.50 percent in early trade on Tuesday from 5.00-5.25 late on Friday on higher commercial bank demand for funds after a long weekend. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah edged lower on selling by some Japanese fund managers, dealers in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  One U.S. bank dealer said big players seemed to have driven spot dollar up to 2,332.5 from its early low of 2,330.5. \par \par \uc2  "Even Singapore operators are scared of them. They have to follow the big players from Japan," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said there was no fundamental reason for the move but Japanese fund managers appeared to have triggered early dollar buying despite counter selling from local players. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said liquidity was relatively ample but U.S. fund managers seemed to be supporting the rupiah due to its attractive yield. \par \par \uc2  "There is not much direction in the market. The signals are a bit mixed," one said, adding that swap rates were a bit high although liquidity was ample. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was locked within a 10-point band in thin trade, with wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support for the U.S. dollar limiting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside. \par \par \uc2  A European bank dealer said dollar/Sing was being supported by commercial buying interest around the 1.4050 level, while sell orders around 1.4060 were capping gains. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said U.S. dollar purchases by local banks, which normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), was too light to shift dollar/Sing out of its range. \par \par \uc2  "They are not really pushing the price. I would say they are not acting on behalf of the MAS," said a senior Japanese bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to support the U.S. dollar on any moves towards S$1.4040 have kept the market from pushing up the Singapore unit in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  -- Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221-384-6364), Tang Nguyen in Bangkok (662-252-9950), Madhav Reddy and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703080) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds4909mzw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
