\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Ringgit falls on speculative sales. \b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 11 (Reuter) - Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were softer on Monday following the U.S. dollar's strong gains after the release of a sharply higher-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls report. \par \par \uc2  The dollar gained ground in Tokyo after rising in New York on Friday on news that February payrolls had risen by 705,000, more than double the level projected by economists. \par \par \uc2  In Malaysia, the ringgit slid on dollar buying by a large Japanese speculator in early morning Sydney. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese buying was purely speculative, but it triggered several stops," a dealer with a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  He said the action had ceased in late morning Kuala Lumpur and the ringgit was likely to remain in a range for the day. \par \par \uc2  "The market is looking at the seven percent tender from (Bank) Negara as being indicative that Negara is not going to follow dollar interest rates," said a chief dealer with a U.S. bank in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank Negara Malaysia said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market to borrow for three-month deposit at 7.0 percent, unchanged from the last time it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in response to a massive speculative attack on the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar held firm despite the U.S. dollar's rise and a sharp drop on the local bourse following the Dow's plunge on Friday due to the payrolls report. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being bolstered by ringgit/Sing cross sales and possible nervousness over China's missile tests near Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  "The residual Sing strength at the current levels is possibly still a part of the Taiwan factor," said the U.S. bank dealer. "But it's hard to justify an investment in the Singapore market where you get neither a return on the money market and a potential downward move in equities," he added. \par \par \uc2  A European bank dealer said Taiwanese funds were more likely to invest in Singapore property than in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  or stock markets. "It's the most stable and there's only one way property can go," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah dipped amid ample liquidity during early trade but showed little discernible trend, Jakarta dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A foreign bank dealer said some players were seen trying to test the high side of the dollar, but the market had no firm direction with major volume coming from offshore. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate at 2,303/2,369 and the conversion rate at 2,303/2,347 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's firmer tone put pressure on the Thai baht, which fell to 25.230-25.239 against 25.215-25.218, despite tightened money market conditions. \par \par \uc2  The Thai overnight interbank rate rose to 7.00 percent in early Monday trade from 6.50-6.75 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The money supply tightened slightly as some banks were short of funds after the weekend," said one foreign bank dealer in Bangkok. "But I don't think the interbank rate will rise much today. The funds in the market remain plentiful." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the mid-rate at 25.24 to the dollar versus 25.21 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The one-month swap premium rose to 6.50/7.00 satangs in early trade from 6.25-6.75 late on Friday on talk that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was studying a plan to impose a tax on dollar swap for baht transactions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "That talk prompted heavy swap transactions. They think that it is better to buy now to avoid any possible loss if such a tax were to be really imposed," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. \par \par \uc2  -- Madhav Reddy in KL (603-230 8911), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Tang Nguyen in Bangkok (662-252-9950) and Sonali Desai in Singapore (65-8703093) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds3b00c6x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
