\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore/Budget/Market Impact -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Concerns \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 1996\par 08:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimate that a cut in the corporate tax rate to 26% from 27% could add 1.5 percentage points to their forecasts of corporate earnings growth of around 18.5% in 1996. \par \par \uc2  The budget will also confirm the strength of state finances. Economists forecast the surplus for the 1996 fiscal year will be near $10 billion (Singapore) against a government estimate a year ago of S$5.75 billion. \par \par \uc2  Yang Sy Jian, economist At Kay Hian James Capel Ltd., said one remedy the government might consider in the budget to offset the 'contractionary' impact of the surplus is to top up individual Singaporeans' contributions to the Central Provident Fund (CPF), a state-run pension fund. \par \par \uc2  The strength of state finances feeds the Singapore dollar's strength against the U.S. dollar, which is a good reason for government to cut taxes, some economists said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de factor \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , propped up the dollar when it dipped below S$1.4000 last week. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 0.6% in value against the local unit since the end of 1995. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar has proved steady this week against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trading at S$1.4075. \par \par \uc2  Economists noted the MAS, which uses exchange-rate policy as its key monetary policy tool, will be anxious to curb any further appreciation in the Singapore dollar in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Foregoing tax revenue by easing the fiscal burden on companies, and encouraging consumer demand for imports through lower personal taxation - especially when government forecasts inflation to remain a low 1.7% in 1996 - would be ways for government to help curb the Singapore dollar's upward momentum, said UBS Global Research economist Chia Woon Khien. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-02-96 \par \par \uc2  0711GMT \par \par \uc2  1147 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds2r01ree\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
