\par\par \b \uc2  MAS buys dlr for BOJ at 104.50-60 yen and above. \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 23 (Reuter) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) bought dollars at 104.50-60 yen and above on behalf of the Bank of Japan (BOJ) on Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the MAS entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market three times with dollar bids for yen, starting at 104.50-60 yen. The MAS's dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in relatively small lots, the traders said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS's move was followed by BOJ dollar buying of $500 million at 104.80 yen, they said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a low of 104.47 yen in Asian trade, compared with 104.95/00 yen in Tokyo at 9 a.m. on Friday (0000 GMT) and against its New York close of 104.95/05 yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) and the Bank of Japan unveiled a pact which allows Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ask its counterparts in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hong Kong to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf in dollar/yen trade in their markets. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the MAS's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar/yen trade on the BOJ's behalf on Friday, a senior MOF official said: "No comment." \par \par \uc2  An official at the BOJ said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been in close contact with other central banks on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, but declined further comment. \par \par \uc2  A MAS spokesman also declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  In announcing the new pact on Tuesday, the MOF and BOJ said in a statement: "In view of rapid development of Asian and Oceanian financial markets, cooperation among Asian and Oceanian monetary authorities in the market is increasingly important." \par \par \uc2  The statement added: "Against this background, monetary authorities of Hong Kong, Singapore and Japan recently formed arrangements in which Hong Kong and Singaporean authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dollar-yen in their markets on behalf of Japanese authorities." \par \par \uc2  A bank trader in Singapore said: "MAS was buying dollars for yen for the BOJ when the dollar/yen moved up from about 104.60 yen." -- Yoshiko Mori, Tokyo Treasury Desk 813-3432-8676 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011019ds2n0161r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
