\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore dollar seen likely to remain safe haven. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 1996\par 04:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 28 (Reuter) - The inflow of funds from neighbouring countries will continue to boost the Singapore dollar this year despite government concern about its safe haven appeal, dealers and economists said at the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "I think the Sing dollar will continue to be the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region," said a strategist at a large U.S. bank, citing Singapore's low inflation rate, large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and healthy trade as factors underlying investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's secure position was thrown into relief late last week as investors sold the Malaysian ringgit aggressively against the Singapore dollar due to growing concern over a delay in the release of Malaysia's October trade data. \par \par \uc2  The sell-off, which pushed the Singapore dollar to a high of 1.4165 against a surging U.S. dollar, prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers attributed the Singapore dollar's gains late last week partly to nervousness about a possible Chinese attack on Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  Economists said uncertainty in the economic or political climate of neighbouring countries would continue to benefit the "Asian greenback", although it was expected to appreciate at a slower pace this year. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar hit an all-time high of 1.39 to the U.S. dollar last April as investors dumped the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah in a panic over emerging economies after the Mexican peso crashed. \par \par \uc2  Soon after the peso crisis, Finance Minister Richard Hu said Singapore would not hesitate to curb sharp rises in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted by a sudden inflow of funds. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore has attracted its share of safe haven money ... If this persists for any length of time, it causes asset inflation and you got to balance it," Hu said. \par \par \uc2  Most economists said the Singapore dollar would appreciate to about 1.38-1.40 to the U.S. dollar by year end, but there was no immediate consensus on its shorter term outlook. \par \par \uc2  "My guess is that the authorities would be willing to accept a one to two percent appreciation of the Singapore dollar over the course of the year," said Andy Tan, general manager of MMS International. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength over the past three years had helped keep a check on inflation in import-dependent Singapore. \par \par \uc2  But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed signs of rising to 1.35 to the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  Tay Mei Yuen, economist at Crosby Securities, said Singapore's slowing growth and concerns about export competitivenes suggested a slower rate of appreciation for its dollar. \par \par \uc2  Tay said the U.S. dollar was likely to reach S$1.38 by the year end and that the Singapore dollar would appreciate by only 0.5 percent on a trade weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the authorities are targeting merely the U.S. dollar, although that is the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. dollar's going up now but the yen is coming down, and Japan is one of our top export partners," she said. \par \