\par\par \b \uc2  George Soros seen behind ringgit sales - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 25 (Reuter) - The shadow of billionaire American investor George Soros was glimpsed behind a continued attack on the Malaysian ringgit in favour of the Singapore dollar on Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit recovered slightly after Bank Negara Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the U.S. dollar at around 2.5640 in the morning. It was quoted at 2.5605/15 in late afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  "There's no confirmation. But I do think it's the only fund with big enough pockets to fly so aggressively in the face of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Malaysia," said a dealer with a U.K. bank. \par \par \uc2  He said the nature and location of the activity mirrored movements last week, when Soros was also believed to be behind an attack on the ringgit before Bank Negara effectively raised interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian-born Soros is considered one of the world's most successful hedge fund managers. \par \par \uc2  The U.K. bank dealer estimated Soros' Quantum Group of funds had probably sold about one billion ringgit over the past two days. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's status as a safe haven and its relative lack of volatility made it an obvious choice for an investor speculating in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment for the ringgit has turned bearish due to growing anxiety about Malaysia's trade figures. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar benefited from its neighbour's woes for the second straight day on Thursday, firming to around 1.4168/78 to the U.S. dollar in late afternoon trade, despite the greenback's strong tone against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chia Woon Khien, Union Bank of Switzerland's economist for Singapore and Malaysia, said she would not rule out the possibility of Bank Negara raising interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  "It's understandable that the ringgit would weaken given that the U.S. dollar has gained a lot of ground in the last couple of days. But I don't think they (Bank Negara) like the way the market has been speculating," she said. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit made a sharp but short-lived recovery last week after Bank Negara raised three and six-month deposit rates by an average 25 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's rise on Thursday, but it was expected to step in if the U.S. dollar showed signs of slipping below S$1.4150/60. \par \par \uc2  "There's no really fundamental reason for the MAS to want to allow the Singapore dollar to appreciate in this fashion," Chia said. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom (65-8703080) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds1p041j9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
