\par\par \b \uc2  Ringgit sales bolster Singapore dollar --dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 24 (Reuter) - The Singapore dollar held its own despite the U.S. dollar's sharp rise against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, as investors sold the Malaysian ringgit in favour of its calmer neighbour. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers attributed the Singapore dollar's resilience and ringgit sales to growing anxiety about Malaysia's delayed trade figures and disappointment over Bank Negara's failure to raise interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was quoted at 1.4210/20 to the U.S. dollar in late afternoon trade, after firming to 1.4188 earlier. \par \par \uc2  "We have been waiting for the release of Malaysian trade data for a few weeks now. And it really reduces investor confidence if they don't release the figures on time," said the head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  He said a large European bank had been actively selling the ringgit in the past few weeks, possibly in an effort to transfer funds out of Malaysian stocks to the Singapore bourse. \par \par \uc2  "People really feel that unless (Malaysian interest) rates rise substantially,the ringgit is vulnerable, especially against the Singapore dollar," he said, pointing to Singapore's high current account surplus as a reason for confidence in its dollar. \par \par \uc2  The ringgit staged a sharp recovery late last week after Bank Negara raised three and six-month deposit rates by an average 25 basis points. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepted slightly higher bids at its borrowing for three-month deposits, but players failed to react to what they saw as a cosmetic move. \par \par \uc2  "It wasn't a big increase. I think the market's looking for a proper increase in the rates," said a dealer with a U.K. bank. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on two occasions to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from firming beyond S$1.4200. \par \par \uc2  "They were just putting a bid at the (1.42) figure level. They were not really pushing it up higher," said a dealer, adding that the MAS was expected to provide strong backing for the U.S. dollar at S$1.4180. \par \par \uc2  The MAS's presence is normally detected through activity by one of Singapore's major local banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar's gains were likely to be capped at around S$1.4250 in the near term, despite its rise against the Deutschemark and Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore enjoys a huge current account surplus and that will always create pressure for the Singapore dollar to appreciate," said Andy Tan, general manager of MMS International. \par \par \uc2  According to the latest figures, Singapore's current account surplus for third quarter 1995 stood at Singapore S5.76 billion, compared with S$4.61 billion in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said selling of the U.S. dollar against the Singapore unit by Brunei's national investment agency had abated by mid-week. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom (65-8703080) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds1o043rp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
