\par\par \b \uc2  Sterling's surge a mystery to  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Dezember 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 27 (Reuter) - The identity of the trader or financial institution whose purchases of sterling sent the British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply higher against the U.S. dollar overnight remained a mystery on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Singapore and Tokyo agreed that the impact of the orders was boosted by skimpy Christmas holiday volumes. \par \par \uc2  The pound sterling rose to $1.5595/05 at the close of New York trade on Tuesday, up from $1.5435/45 at Friday's U.S. close. It was quoted at around 1.5555/62 in mid-afternoon in Singapore on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sterling's rise in New York was propelled by a series of stop-loss buying orders between $1.5450 and $1.5550 in thin market conditions. Stop-loss orders are instructions to buy or sell which operate only when a given price is reached. \par \par \uc2  "At this time of year, it doesn't take a very large amount to move sterling by this magnitude," said a European bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  He said the purchases could have come from a trader trying to buy up the British \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the end of the year, with an eye to selling it off later. But he said sterling was likely to be vulnerable to profit-taking after its overnight rise against the dollar and mark. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers said the rally was probably sparked by speculative buying by Middle East operators, while one U.K. bank dealer said there were rumours of a purchase by Swiss-based funds or Singapore monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  A local bank dealer in Singapore said it was unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), the city-state's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was behind the sterling purchases. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the MAS would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in sterling. They only come in during the day if they need to, and usually they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Sing (\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar) market," she said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the MAS tended to prop up the U.S. dollar against its Singapore counterpart when the latter showed signs of strengthening too sharply. Its presence was normally seen through activity by major local banks, they said. \par \par \uc2  The local bank dealer said the purchases could have come from a large hedge fund trying to guard against the uncertainty surrounding U.S. budget negotiations, which have kept the U.S. government partially shut for a second consecutive week. \par \par \uc2  She said sterling had been overbought after Tuesday night's move and was unlikely to strengthen above $1.5620 during Asian or European trade. \par \par \uc2  But a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a U.K. bank in Singapore said sterling's move was too small to suggest that an institution or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to sell off U.S. assets out of concern about the budget stalemate. \par \par \uc2  "I would not have classified sterling as having a safe haven status. If people were to move out of the U.S. dollar, the first (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) that would make a big gain should be the Swiss franc. But it has dropped about 30 points since yesterday," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drcr00dk4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
