\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW - Singapore comfortable with strong dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ajoy Sen \par \uc2 245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 1995\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 22 (Reuter) - Singapore is comfortable with the appreciation of the local dollar against major international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Richard Hu said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "For the time being, Singapore is comfortable with the present rate of appreciation," Hu told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  "It is quite stable this year after a rapid run-up last year," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The value of the Singapore dollar is market driven," he said, adding that it was driven basically by Singapore's strong fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Hu said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to ensure there are no large fluctuations due to market manipulations. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated by 2.88 percent against the U.S. dollar since the beginning of this year. It rose five percent aginst the Japanese yen and almost four percent against the British pound over the same period. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the impact the growing strength of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had on higher \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs, Hu said the government recognised the appreciating Singapore dollar had created some problems for the manufacturing and service sectors. \par \par \uc2  "But there were two sides to the coin," he added. \par \par \uc2  Although a strong Singapore dollar raised the selling price of goods produced locally, it reduced the costs of manufacturing and service inputs, he said, adding that approximately 70 percent of Singapore's manufaturing inputs were imported. \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom (65-8703080) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drcm00ayw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
